+\clearpage
+\section{Compact-cell local annealing: implemented method and results}
+\label{sec:compact-cell-annealing}
+
+This section records the method actually executed in the recovered
+\texttt{work/median100/anneal.cpp}, and the bounded Stop/resume adaptation
+\texttt{anneal\_100\_bounded\_control.cpp}. It concerns cell loads in a
+shared-corner hexagonal mesh. The projected pooled-centroid rule discussed
+earlier belongs to the historical vertex-agent experiment.
+
+\subsection{Data, mesh, and initialization}
+The input retains all 5,986 original pickup records, including duplicates.
+The full-resolution NYC DCP26b Manhattan main-island polygon retains its hole.
+Records with zero-based indices 309, 4705, and 5212 lie outside this domain;
+the remaining 5,983 records supply the cell loads. Coordinates are in the
+recovered planar kilometre frame.
+
+The regular initializer searches hexagon side lengths from 0.520 to 0.700 km
+in increments of 0.002 km and six offset pairs. Among meshes with exactly
+100 positive-area domain intersections, it selects the smallest sum of
+squared clipped-shape excesses. The retained regular mesh has side length
+0.558 km, offset fractions $(0.25,0.25)$, and 257 shared vertices.
+Boundary vertices may move; mesh connectivity and the six vertex identifiers
+of each cell remain fixed.
+
+The regular seed does not satisfy the clipped-cell shape bound. A geometry
+repair phase, using seed 7, produced the saved feasible start after one
+repair round. Its geometry is the initial state for balancing (balancing
+round 0); it is not the historical round-151 optimized state.
+
+\subsection{Local activation and displacement proposals}
+Each round shuffles all 257 mesh vertices and visits each once. For active
+vertex $v$, let $U_v$ contain $v$ and its mesh-edge neighbours, and let $C_v$
+contain every cell incident to a vertex in $U_v$. There are 24 proposal
+attempts per activation. Every attempt uses the current committed state.
+The proposed vertex displacements are evaluated and committed together.
+
+For each attempt, sample a displacement scale $d$ logarithmically uniformly
+between $0.0008s$ and $0.45s$, where $s=0.558$ km, and choose one of seven
+modes using the generator's integer output modulo seven. Write $a$ for a
+shared Gaussian displacement and $z_u$ for the individual Gaussian
+displacements, each with independent coordinate standard deviation $d$.
+The proposal modes are:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item Move only the active vertex by $z_v$.
+\item Move $v$ by $a$ and its edge neighbours by $0.65a$.
+\item Move each vertex $u\in U_v$ by $0.7a+0.3z_u$.
+\item Move $v$ by $z_v$; each other vertex independently either moves by
+      $z_u$ or remains fixed, selected by the generator's output modulo two.
+\item Move all vertices in $U_v$ independently by $z_u$.
+\item Use the data-directed displacement described below, with noise
+      coefficient $0.2$.
+\item Use that data-directed displacement with noise coefficient $0.6$.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+For either data-directed mode, choose the least-loaded cell incident to $v$
+whose load is below 54. Compute its vertex-average centre and find the
+nearest pickup among those owned by cells in $C_v$. Replace $a$ by a vector
+of length $d$ toward that pickup when the direction is nonzero. Vertices of
+the chosen cell receive displacement $a+\beta z_u$; the other vertices in
+$U_v$ receive $0.35a+\beta z_u$, with $\beta=0.2$ or $0.6$. If no deficient
+incident cell exists, the mode falls back to moving only $v$.
+
+These formulas describe the proposals; the retained source also fixes the
+order of random draws, including draws whose values are discarded. Exact
+continuation therefore preserves the generator and normal-distribution cache,
+rather than regenerating proposals from the formulas alone.
+
+\subsection{Reallocation and feasibility}
+Pool the pickups currently owned by the affected cells $C_v$, then assign
+each to the first containing proposed cell in increasing cell-identifier
+order. Initial ownership also uses the first containing cell in that order.
+The half-plane membership test uses tolerance $10^{-10}$ in squared
+kilometre units. If any pooled pickup has no owner, reject the proposal.
+All other pickup owners remain unchanged.
+
+For every affected underlying hexagon, require strict convexity, area at
+least 2\% of its original regular area, and diameter/minimum-width ratio
+at most 2. During balancing, require the clipped service-region ratio at
+most 2 as well. The implementation computes diameter and minimum projection
+width through the convex hull, which preserves both quantities for the
+clipped set. In geometry repair, the clipped ratio is instead the repair
+objective, so that initially infeasible clipped shapes can improve.
+
+For proposals involving outer-boundary vertices, additionally reject a
+self-intersecting outer boundary or uncovered domain area exceeding
+$10^{-10}$ km$^2$. Strictly convex shared faces and boundary checks preserve
+the intended mesh embedding; the implementation does not perform an
+exhaustive pairwise overlap test on every proposal. Independent saved-state
+audits explicitly check underlying and clipped overlaps, full coverage,
+100 positive-area service regions, and unique pickup assignment.
+
+Only after all acceptance and feasibility checks pass are positions and
+ownership committed atomically. Equal-energy proposals undergo the same
+geometric checks as other proposals.
+
+\subsection{Balancing energy and local annealing acceptance}
+Let $n_i$ denote cell load and define the affected-cell energy
+\[
+ E_v=\sum_{i\in C_v}n_i^2.
+\]
+Since the affected cells conserve their total pickup count, its change
+equals the change in the global squared-load sum. For a fixed total load,
+minimizing that sum is equivalent to minimizing load variance.
+
+Set
+\[
+ \ell=\left\lceil0.9\frac{5983}{100}\right\rceil=54,
+ \qquad u=\left\lfloor1.1\frac{5983}{100}\right\rfloor=65,
+ \qquad e_i=\max\{\ell-n_i,0,n_i-u\}.
+\]
+For the current patch, put $Q_v=\sum_{i\in C_v}e_i^2$. If $Q_v=0$, skip
+the proposal attempt. Otherwise use
+\[
+ T_v=0.02\max\{Q_v,0.01\},\qquad
+ p_{\mathrm{accept}}=
+ \begin{cases}
+  1,& E_v^{\mathrm{new}}\le E_v^{\mathrm{old}},\\
+  \exp\bigl(-(E_v^{\mathrm{new}}-E_v^{\mathrm{old}})/T_v\bigr),
+     & E_v^{\mathrm{new}}>E_v^{\mathrm{old}}.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+Acceptance remains conditional on feasibility. The band $[54,65]$ controls
+the local temperature and deficient-cell direction; it is not a load
+constraint or a termination criterion. There is no cooling schedule:
+the coefficient 0.02 is fixed, while temperature depends on current local
+loads. Balancing uses seed 17.
+
+In the repair phase, replace the cell energy by
+\[
+ E_i^{\mathrm{repair}}=
+ \bigl(\max\{r(S_i)-1.98,0\}\bigr)^2,
+\]
+where $r$ is the diameter/minimum-width ratio. Use the affected-cell repair
+energy in place of $Q_v$ and the load energy in the acceptance formula.
+Repair ends after a full round with total repair energy below $10^{-12}$;
+the resulting geometry is audited before balancing.
+
+\subsection{What the termination score does and does not measure}
+The median-of-local-median neighbour gap $M$ is defined in
+Section~\ref{sec:compact-hexagon-protocol}, with service-border adjacency,
+the focal cell's load as denominator, and the stated zero-load convention.
+It is evaluated after completed balancing rounds. Success requires the
+first such round with $M\le5\%$.
+
+\textbf{The acceptance energy is squared load, whereas the stopping score
+is the median neighbour gap.} A move reducing squared load can increase
+$M$, and annealing can also accept increases in squared load. Thus neither
+$M$ nor squared load is monotone in this execution. The protocol supplies
+no proof that the 5\% condition will eventually be reached. Even attaining
+that median condition would not establish good balance for every cell.
+
